course: world
lesson: 165
title: The brother who never does anything wrong: double standards at home
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Mum always says, "Oliver would never lie." Oliver says he did his homework, and the sheet in his bag is blank. Mum: "He must have done it on a different sheet." The evidence says one thing, and Mum invents an excuse so her belief can stay.
explain: A double standard is judging the same thing differently depending on who did it. Ben breaks a vase: "He's only little." Hannah broke a cup at the same age: "That was different, you were naughty." Same act, same age, two judgements.
explain: Special pleading is a rule for everyone except me: "No phones at the table," says Dad, then checks his own: "That's different, it's work." Or: "Sugar is bad," says Dad, eating a doughnut, "but grown-ups need energy."
explain: Watch out for "always" and "never". Tom says Amy is "always" late; she was late once this month and Tom four times. Count first, then speak: "You were late today" is fair and true.
explain: The mockery aims at the unfairness, not the person: "Oh, the invisible sheet. The famous invisible sheet." A fair rule is one that would still sound fair if you swapped the names.
fact: A double standard means judging the same act or result differently depending on who did it.
fact: Special pleading means making an exception to a rule for yourself or a favourite without a real reason.
fact: The swap test: if a rule or judgement stops sounding fair when you swap the names, it is a double standard.
fact: Inventing a new excuse whenever the evidence goes against your belief is called moving the goalposts.
fact: Calling every example that breaks your rule "an exception" is a trap; grown-ups call it "no true Scotsman".
fact: "Always" and "never" are almost never true about people; count the times before using them.
fact: Age can be a fair reason to be gentle, but then it must be fair for every child at that age.
fact: If a rule has a good reason, like talking together at dinner, it applies to everyone; a real exception, like an emergency, should be said in advance for everyone.
fact: Sugar is fine in small amounts and too much harms teeth and bodies at any age.
fact: Screens can be fine or too much for anyone of any age; what matters is how long and what you watch.
fact: Same mark, same praise: effort can be talked about separately and honestly.
fact: Words people use: "make it make sense" means this makes no sense (young slang), "sit down" means you're in no position to judge, "doughnut man" is a jokey nickname, "The Chase" is a TV quiz show in Britain, "wanna" means want to, and "bossy" here is a joke about gum giving orders.
exercise: Mum says Oliver would never lie, and his homework sheet is blank. What does she do? | Invents an excuse: he did it on a different sheet | Checks with his teacher straight away | Gives him a week with no telly | Asks Oliver to show her the other sheet, and then phones his teacher
exercise: Dad says no phones at dinner, then checks his own: "That's different, it's work." What is this called? | Special pleading, a rule for everyone except me | Moving the goalposts | A fair exception said in advance | Going in circles
exercise: Ben breaks a vase and is excused for being little; Hannah broke a cup at his age and was told off. What is the trap? | A double standard: same act, same age, different judgement | A fair rule because Ben is younger | A hasty generalisation about vases | An appeal to tradition
exercise: Grandma says young people are always on their phones, and she watches quiz shows two hours a day. What is wrong? | She blames others for what she does herself | Quiz shows are not screens | Young people never use their phones, so she must be wrong about them | Two hours is not much time
exercise: The school says Liam stuck gum under a desk. Dad says someone must have made him. What is Dad doing? | Shifting the blame to save his belief | Finding out exactly what really happened | Punishing Liam fairly for what he really did | Thanking the school for calling him so quickly
exercise: Mum promises the beach if the weather is good, then adds "not windy", then "not too hot". What is she doing? | Adding a new condition every time one is met | Keeping her promise exactly | Checking the weather forecast fairly | Giving the real reason, which is that she is too tired to go today
exercise: Tom says Amy is always late. How often was each late this month? | Amy once, Tom four times | Amy four times, Tom once | Both of them every day | Neither of them at all
exercise: Dad says sweets are bad, eating a doughnut: "Grown-ups need energy." What is true? | Too much sugar is bad at any age, and kids need energy too | Grown-ups need sugar for energy, but children don't need it at all | Doughnuts are not really sweets | Sugar only harms children's teeth
exercise: Twins Lucy and Lola both got 7 out of 10, but Mum praises only Lucy. What is fair? | Same score, same praise; talk about effort separately | Praise whichever twin is older | Praise the twin who is quieter | Give Lola a lower mark next time, so the twins can be told apart
exercise: Grandpa says kids don't respect elders, then calls Jamal, who helped all evening, "an exception". What is this trap called? | "No true Scotsman", calling every counterexample an exception | Appeal to tradition | Asking a fair question | A hasty generalisation about shopping
exercise: What is the swap test? | Swap the names and see if it still sounds fair | Swap seats at the dinner table and see who complains | Swap the rules around every week so everyone gets a turn | Swap the punishment for a reward and see who behaves
exercise: When is an exception to a rule fair? | When the reason is real and said in advance for everyone | Whenever the person in charge needs one, for any reason at all | Whenever it is a grown-up | Whenever the rule feels annoying
exercise: Why is "he never does anything wrong" a risky belief for a parent? | You will miss it when he does something wrong | It makes him do everything wrong | It is always true for the youngest child | It makes the other children in the family behave much better
exercise: Which word should make you count before you believe it? | "Always" | "Sometimes" | "Today" | "Once"
exercise: What really matters about screen time, at any age? | How long and what you watch | Only whether you are young | Only whether it is a quiz show | Only the size of the screen
test: Big sister: "Oh, the invisible sheet. The famous invisible sheet. When I forgot mine it was a week of no telly." What two things is she pointing out? | Mum's made-up excuse, and a different rule for her | That Oliver can make things invisible | That she likes telly more than homework | That homework sheets are often lost
test: Kid: "Oh, so it's different when it's you? That's crazy, Dad." Is this rude about Dad as a person? | No, it calls out the rule he broke, not who he is | Yes, it calls Dad crazy as a person, which is rude | Yes, because kids may never question their parents' rules | No, because it agrees with Dad's rule about phones
test: Hannah: "I was four. He's four. Make it make sense." Gran: "She's not wrong, Sue." What is Gran agreeing with? | The same act at the same age should be judged the same way | Ben should be punished more than Hannah ever was, because vases cost more | Hannah was naughty when she was four | Vases are worth more than cups
test: Teen: "Nan, you've watched The Chase three times today. That's a screen, Nan." Nan: "That's educational." What trap is Nan in? | A made-up exception for her own screen time | Watching too little television | Thinking quiz shows are bad | Being far too strict with herself about watching any television
test: Liam's sister: "Yeah, the gum forced him. Gum is very bossy." What is she being sarcastic about? | Dad blaming anything but Liam | Liam being too bossy at school | Chewing gum being really dangerous | The school phoning home too often
test: Kid: "Mum, at this rate the weather has to sign a contract. Just say you don't wanna go." What would an honest Mum say? | "I'm tired today, let's go next week." | "The weather isn't good enough yet." | "We'll go when it's not windy, hot or cold." | "Beaches are always bad luck."
test: Amy: "Bro, you've been late more than me, like, this WEEK. Sit down." What does "sit down" mean here? | You're in no position to judge me | Please take a seat | Let's sit and talk calmly | You're tired, have a rest
test: What would happen if Dad swapped names in his rule: "Kids can't have sweets, but kids need energy"? | He'd see the rule only works when it suits him | He'd prove that sugar is good for kids | The rule would become fairer for grown-ups | Nothing, because rules don't change with names
test: Lola: "Same. Number. Seven equals seven, Mum, I checked." Which is the fair answer from Mum? | "You're right, you both did well. Let's talk about revising." | "Lucy's seven is worth a bit more than yours, because she's older." | "Sevens are different for different people, that's just how it is." | "You should have got an eight for it to count, so try harder."
test: Jamal: "Grandpa, every kid you meet is 'an exception'. Maybe your rule's the exception." What does this show? | If lots of cases break a rule, the rule is probably wrong | Grandpa only meets unusual children | Exceptions prove that rules are true, so Grandpa's rule is still right | Jamal doesn't respect his grandpa
test: In a cartoon the king says, "Nobody may eat cake," and eats cake: "Kings don't count." Put it right. | It's special pleading; a fair rule applies to the one who makes it | Kings really are allowed any exception | It's fine because the cake was his | It's fair as long as he says it loudly
exam: Identify the principal defect in a judgement whereby an identical act, committed at an identical age, is excused in one child and censured in another. | It constitutes a double standard | It constitutes an appeal to tradition | It constitutes a controlled experiment | It constitutes a fair and reasonable exception
exam: Ascertain which procedure most reliably reveals whether a rule is applied impartially. | Substitution of the names of the persons concerned | Measurement of the volume at which the rule is stated | Enquiry into the age of the rule | Repetition of the rule on a daily basis
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the frequency of lateness of the two siblings in the present month. | The sister was late once and the brother four times | The sister was late on every occasion | The brother was never late | Both were late an equal number of times
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the practice of designating every contrary example as "an exception"? | It shields a rule from evidence that it is false | It confirms the rule beyond doubt | It is a fair method of counting | It increases the accuracy of the rule with every new example
